# Awareness rules — resurfacing the frame

> **Reminders, not gates.** Nothing here blocks anything. An agent deep in task 14 has not
> *disobeyed* the plan; it has lost sight of it, because its context is full of task 14. You do
> not fix that by denying a tool call. You fix it by putting the frame back in front of the agent
> at the moments it is most likely to have gone.

## The one rule that decides whether any of this works

**A reminder that repeats is wallpaper. A reminder that reports is news.**

Static text — the rules file pasted in again, the same banner every turn — stops being read after
the third firing. Worse, it teaches the agent that injected text is skippable, which degrades the
reminders that *do* matter.

So every reminder here is **computed from current state**: task statuses parsed from disk, the
commit log, the changelog. Then the message differs every time it fires, because the situation
differs, and it keeps being read for the same reason a status line is read and a splash screen is
not.

This also makes it cheap: one script, no model, no tokens, one source of truth feeding several
surfaces.

---

## Four registers

Firing one reminder everywhere is the mistake. Three of these differ by **weight** and all point
at the agent. The fourth differs by **audience** and points the other way.

### 1 · Ambient — one line, constantly

```
Wave 2 · T14 search-guard · 6/20 done · 1 blocked (T09)
```

Position only. **No rules in this register** — hard rules repeated every turn become wallpaper
within an hour and take the ambient line down with them. This exists so the agent is never more
than one line away from knowing where it is.

### 2 · Situational — at the moment of relevance

Triggered by what the agent is about to do, and **non-blocking**:

> *note: this file is owned by T12 — the fragment protocol applies here*

This is how the ownership rule gets teeth without the harness ever saying no. It is never noise,
because it only fires when it is the relevant fact. A situational reminder that fires when it is
not relevant is an ambient reminder wearing a disguise, and should be demoted.

### 3 · Punctuated — the full picture, rarely, at boundaries

```
WHERE:     Wave 2 of 4 · T14 in progress
CLOSED:    T11, T12 verified · T13 claimed, ungated
IN FLIGHT: T14 (you), T15 (migrator)
BLOCKED:   T09 — awaiting decision D4
CHANGED:   plan amended twice since kickoff → CHANGELOG
NEXT:      T16 gates on T14; wave 3 gates on all of wave 2
```

This is the institutional step-back — the thing standups and retrospectives exist to force in
human teams. It is where hard rules belong, because a boundary is when the frame is being rebuilt
anyway.

Note where **CHANGED** comes from: the amendment receipts required by principle 13. That
mechanism was built for exactly this feed.

Note also the split in **CLOSED**. A status line reading `DONE` is a *claim*; the method's rule
that every "done" be a re-runnable command is what turns it into evidence. Report the two
separately or the surface quietly becomes a certificate the worker issued to itself.

### 4 · Silent — for the requester, not the agent

The three above cost the agent context. This one costs it nothing, because the agent never sees
it: a progress record written where the person who asked for the work can read it without
asking.

Phase 10 already requires *"a status surface the requester can read without asking — published,
not narrated."* This is the thing that maintains it.

Keep it mute for two reasons. It is free — no tokens, no interruption. And an agent that gets
acknowledged for updating its status starts producing status updates *performatively*; silence
keeps the line a record rather than a performance.

## The moments

Event names below are the common ones; **confirm the injection contract for each against your
harness's hook documentation** before wiring, since what a hook may return differs per event.

Note first that almost every available event is **harness-shaped, not work-shaped.** A turn
ending means a turn ended. A process exiting means a process exited. None of them means *"a unit
of work completed"*, because the harness has no concept of one.

**The obligation manufactures the missing event.** Principle 13 already requires the status line
to be updated in place as work proceeds. That makes a write to a task file the one observable
act that coincides with a real work boundary — so hooking the write gives you the event the
harness never offered. The discipline that keeps the plan true is the same discipline that
generates the checkpoints; you are not adding a second obligation to get them.

It pays a second time in measurement. Status transitions are timestamped by the filesystem, so
`NOT STARTED → IN PROGRESS → DONE` yields real elapsed time per task and per wave, with nobody
running a benchmark. That is the substrate comparison phase 8 asks for, collected as a byproduct
of working.

| Moment | Register | Why this instant |
|---|---|---|
| Session start | punctuated | cold or warm start — **and re-fires after a compaction, which is the backstop that measurably held 3/3** |
| **Before compaction** | **punctuated** | **inject into the summary — pairs with the register below** |
| A worker finishes | punctuated | one unit closed; the cheapest moment to zoom out |
| **A status line is written** | **silent** | **the only work-shaped event you have; costs the agent nothing** |
| Turn ends | thin check | *does the status line reflect what you just did?* |
| Every turn | ambient | position, one line |
| Before a tool call | situational | ownership, contended files, the deployed checkout |

### The compaction boundary — inject before, re-establish after

Compaction is **literally the instant of forgetting**, and that part is confirmed. What was wrong
was the mechanism claim: an earlier version of this file said the pre-compaction hook was the only
one that mattered and that wiring it alone was enough. The first pilot measured otherwise.

Across three observed compactions:

| Mechanism | Held the frame |
|---|---|
| A register re-fired **after** the boundary, on the post-compact context | **3 of 3** |
| Injection **into** the summary, surviving verbatim | **1 of 2** |

The design intent does work — one summary contained the injected `WHERE:` line verbatim, which is
exactly the point of firing before. But **you cannot rely on the summariser carrying your lines
through.** It carried them once and dropped them once, and nothing in the injection controls which.

So treat the two as **one system, not a ranking**:

- **Before the boundary** — inject state, so the summary has the chance to keep it.
- **After the boundary** — re-establish it, because half the time the summary did not.

The recovery half exists for free in most harnesses: a session-start register re-fires on the
post-compact context. In the pilot the mid-run compaction had *no* pre-compaction hook wired at
all, and the frame still held — the post-boundary register carried it, and the agent's first turn
afterwards correctly knew a commit hash, that two adversarial passes were in flight, and a
never-ship rule. The backstop did the work the headline mechanism was supposed to do.

Wire both. If you are forced to choose, the observed evidence favours the one that fires **after**
— which is the opposite of what this file used to say.

> **The instrument is not exempt.** In the pilot the status hook first looped, and its position
> computation was wrong twice before a receipt-count heuristic fixed it. A reminder that reports
> the wrong position confidently is worse than no reminder, and it will not announce itself. Test
> the reporter against fixtures before trusting what it says.

