## What the state script must do

One script, one mode per register, called from several events. Requirements are mostly about
not being harmful:

- **Never fail.** Exit zero on every path. A reminder that breaks a tool call is worse than no
  reminder at all.
- **Never be slow.** No network. No expensive history walks. It runs constantly.
- **Degrade out loud.** If there are no task files yet, say so in one line. Silence is
  indistinguishable from "everything is fine."
- **Never touch the plan.** It reports state; it does not maintain it. Its only writes are to its
  own log and the surface it publishes — never the plan, the task files or the changelog. The
  moment a reminder starts editing the work, you no longer know who moved it.
- **Report claims as claims.** A status of `DONE` is what a worker asserted. Mark it verified only
  when the task's own done-command actually ran. A surface that blurs the two is a certificate the
  worker issued to itself.
- **Stay boring.** No cleverness, no summarising, no model calls. Its whole value is that it
  reports what is literally on disk.

See [`awareness.sh.template`](awareness.sh.template) for a starting implementation, and
[`hooks.json.template`](hooks.json.template) for the wiring. Start with the pre-compaction hook
alone: it is the highest-value one, it cannot break anything, and confirming one injection
actually lands is worth more than wiring five that might not.

The situational register reads the phase-7 contended-file list — the `OWNERS` file described in
[`_WORKTREES.md`](_WORKTREES.md.template). Without one it is a no-op, which is the correct
failure: no ownership map, no ownership claims.

---

## Do NOT

- Do NOT block anything from these hooks. Awareness and enforcement are different jobs; a
  reminder that can deny is an enforcement mechanism with a friendly tone, and it will be
  designed as one within a week.
- Do NOT put rules in the ambient register.
- Do NOT emit an identical message twice in a row. If the state has not changed, the ambient line
  is enough.
- Do NOT let the script write to the plan, the status files, or the changelog.
- Do NOT let the published surface imply verification it does not have. A self-reported `DONE`
  rendered identically to a gated one is worse than no surface, because it will be trusted.
- Do NOT echo the silent register back to the agent. Acknowledgement turns a record into a
  performance.
- Do NOT add a register because a moment *exists*. Every reminder competes for the same
  attention; adding one makes the others cheaper to ignore.

---

## Why this is not enforcement

It would be easy to read this file and build blocking hooks instead — they are simpler, and they
*feel* stronger.

They are not, for this problem. The failure being addressed is not disobedience, it is
**attentional narrowing**: a worker is doing task 14 correctly and has lost the shape of the plan
around it. Blocking a tool call does not restore that shape; it just stops the one action the
harness happened to be able to pattern-match, while the frame stays gone.

Enforcement has its place — file ownership, the deployed checkout, secrets — and that place is
[`_RULES.md`](_RULES.md). This file is the other half: keeping the agent oriented, so the rules
have something to be obeyed *in service of*.
